<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#indian-ocean-trade">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Ocean trade</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> trade refers to the maritime exchange of goods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles, gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and ceramics across the Indian Ocean, driven by seasonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds. It connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. It enriched states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> suffered when Portuguese intervention disrupted its share.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliphate's revenues derived from agricultural taxation across Mesopotamia's highly productive Sawad, customs duties on the <b>Indian Ocean trade</b> passing through the Persian Gulf, and remittances from provincial governors across an empire stretching from North Africa to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Walashma state in the eastern lowlands of Adal, with its seat at Dakkar near Harar and the port of Zeila as its commercial outlet, a strategic choice that placed the new sultanate further from Ethiopian power while preserving access to the lucrative Red Sea and <b>Indian Ocean trade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aksumite economy was fundamentally a commercial enterprise of extraordinary geographic reach, built on Aksum's position as the entrepôt linking the African interior to the <b>Indian Ocean trade</b> network.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pandya ports, above all Kayal at the mouth of the Tamraparni, were busy centres of the <b>Indian Ocean trade</b>, exporting pearls, cotton cloth and spices and receiving Chinese silk and porcelain and, above all, horses from Arabia and the Persian Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The revival of the <b>Indian Ocean trade</b> at the end of the first millennium brought larger settlements and greater social complexity, and from the eleventh century the Swahili of the coast acted as middlemen for Somali, Egyptian, Nubian, Arab, Persian and Indian traders, built walled towns and coral…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Economic disruption deepened in the early 16th century as the Portuguese contested the <b>Indian Ocean trade</b>, defeating a Mamluk fleet at Diu in 1509 and depriving the sultanate of vital customs revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
